UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 2, Lesson 2: Painting with mixed colours', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"When you paint, how you hold and move the brush changes the marks you make. A flat stroke with the side of the brush covers a wide area, a tip stroke makes a fine line and dabbing with a nearly dry brush makes a rough texture. Mix colours on a palette, not on your picture, and rinse the brush between colours so they stay clean. Cover your table, wear a smock and wash up carefully. Light colours go on first and dark details are added last.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nYou are painting a field of flowers under a blue sky. Plan which strokes and mixed colours you will use for the sky, the flowers and the stems, and in what order.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for flowers.'' Large light areas come first, then details, and each colour is mixed on the palette.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so use light pressure and let layers dry when you want sharp edges.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPaint a landscape of your choice with at least three mixed colours and three types of brush stroke. On the back write the recipe for each mixed colour and which stroke made which part. If you do not have paint, use watered-down crayon or coloured pencil.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"When you paint, how you hold and move the brush changes the marks you make. A flat stroke with the side of the brush covers a wide area, a tip stroke makes a fine line and dabbing with a nearly dry brush makes a rough texture. Mix colours on a palette, not on your picture, and rinse the brush between colours so they stay clean. Cover your table, wear a smock and wash up carefully. Light colours go on first and dark details are added last.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Paint a landscape of your choice with at least three mixed colours and three types of brush stroke. On the back write the recipe for each mixed colour and which stroke made which part. If you do not have paint, use water Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for flowers.'' Large light areas come first,… Reasoning: I used a brush stroke by attending to wet and dry rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so… Correction: Strong answer: ''Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a brush stroke.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so use light pressure and let layers dry when you want sharp edges. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a brush stroke on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a brush stroke, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for… This matches the lesson skill for a brush stroke.","options":["Strong answer: ''Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for…","Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"You are painting a field of flowers under a blue sky. Plan which strokes and mixed colours you will use for the sky, the flowers and the stems, and in what order. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so… That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so…","When you paint, how you hold and move the brush changes the marks you make. A flat stroke with the side of the brush covers a wide area, a tip stroke makes a…","Strong answer: ''Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for…","Careful use of wet and dry with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a brush stroke?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named wet and dry, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the wet and dry used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising a brush stroke. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Strong answer: ''Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for…","Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a brush stroke?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so…","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a brush stroke.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about wet and dry?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so…","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: Bo paints a sunset. He mixes orange from red and yellow, then lays a wide flat stroke across the sky. He adds a little more red…"],"question":"Using this lesson idea — When you paint, how you hold and move the brush changes the marks you make. A flat stroke with the side of… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so…","Paint a landscape of your choice with at least three mixed colours and three types of brush stroke. On the back write the recipe for each mixed…","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a brush stroke?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a brush stroke on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for flowers.'' Large light areas come first,… Reasoning: I used a brush stroke by attending to wet and dry rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a brush stroke, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so… Correction: Strong answer: ''Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a brush stroke.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w02-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a brush stroke.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use a brush stroke with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify wet and dry and explain why it matters.","I can practise a mixed colour independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a palette needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"When you paint, how you hold and move the brush changes the marks you make. A flat stroke with the side of the brush covers a wide area, a tip stroke makes a fine line and dabbing with a nearly dry brush makes a rough texture. Mix colours on a palette, not on your picture, and…","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","When you paint, how you hold and move the brush changes the marks you make. A flat stroke with the side of the brush covers a wide area, a tip stroke makes a fine line and dabbing with a nearly dry brush makes a rough texture. Mix colours on a palette, not on your picture, and rinse the brush between colours so they stay clean. Cover your table, wear a smock and wash up carefully. Light colours go on first and dark details are added last.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a brush stroke?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Bo paints a sunset. He mixes orange from red and yellow, then lays a wide flat stroke across the sky. He adds a little more red at the top, blends the two while they are wet and finishes with a thin dark purple tip stroke for the hills. Light areas first, dark details last.\n\nTRY FIRST\nYou are painting a field of flowers under a blue sky. Plan which strokes and mixed colours you will use for the sky, the flowers and the stems, and in what order.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for flowers.'' Large light areas come first, then details, and each colour is mixed on the palette.","explain":"When you paint, how you hold and move the brush changes the marks you make. A flat stroke with the side of the brush covers a wide area, a tip stroke makes a fine line and dabbing with a nearly dry brush makes a rough texture. Mix colours on a palette, not on your picture, and rinse the brush between colours so they stay clean. Cover your table, wear a smock and wash up carefully. Light colours go on first and dark details are added last.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so use light pressure and let layers dry when you want sharp edges. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Flat and tip brush strokes.","caption":"Choose a stroke for each part.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Flat stroke</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">wide sky area</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Tip stroke</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">thin stems</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Bo paints a sunset. He mixes orange from red and yellow, then lays a wide flat stroke across the sky. He adds a Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Bo paints a sunset. He mixes orange from red and yellow, then lays a wide flat stroke across the sky. He adds a Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a brush stroke?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Bo paints a sunset. He mixes orange from red and yellow, then lays a wide flat stroke across the sky. He adds a little more red at the top, blends the two while they are wet and finishes with a thin dark purple tip stroke for the hills. Light areas first, dark details last. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Order of painting.","caption":"Light colours, then dark.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Light first</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">sky and ground</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Dark last</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">small details</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a brush stroke?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so use light pressure and let layers dry when you want sharp edges. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Bo paints a sunset. He mixes orange from red and yellow, then lays a wide flat stroke across the sky. He adds a little more red at the top, blends the two while they are wet and finishes with a thin dark purple tip stroke for the hills. Light areas first, dark details last.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for flowers.'' Large light areas come first, then details, and each colour is mixed on the palette.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so use light pressure and let layers dry when you want sharp edges. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a brush stroke?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWhen you paint, how you hold and move the brush changes the marks you make. A flat stroke with the side of the brush covers a wide area, a tip stroke makes a fine line and dabbing with a nearly dry brush makes a rough texture. Mix colours on a palette, not on your picture, and rinse the brush between colours so they stay clean. Cover your table, wear a smock and wash up carefully. Light colours go on first and dark details are added last. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 2, Lesson 2: Painting with mixed colours","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Drawing, line, shape and colour","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Bo paints a sunset. He mixes orange from red and yellow, then lays a wide flat stroke across the sky. He adds a little more red at the top, blends the two while they are wet and finishes with a thin dark purple tip stroke for the hills. Light areas first, dark details last.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Flat and tip brush strokes.","caption":"Choose a stroke for each part.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Flat stroke</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">wide sky area</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Tip stroke</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">thin stems</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Order of painting.","caption":"Light colours, then dark.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Light first</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">sky and ground</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Dark last</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">small details</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w02-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
